00:00:00.719 --> 00:00:02.790 align:start position:0%
 
first person shooters are awesome  they're action-packed games backed by  incredible graphics  and if i wanted to quickly make an fps  there's some really great options  you've got easy tutorials in unreal  engine and unity even comes with a fully  featured fps sample that works right out  of the box but what if you wanted to  build one using something else weirdly  enough anything javascript related isn't  really that high or even present on the  list of engines that you might use  i feel like i can make it work though  first person camera systems are  conceptually pretty simple  you've just got the camera wedged in the  guys or the girl's chest and every  movement happens relative to your  orientation  i started by working on getting simple  camera movement working first  it doesn't take much to get a simple  scene with simple camera movement going  the devil's in the details though can  play around quite a bit with getting  little details in like that head bobbing  effect it's just a sine wave that you  apply to the camera as you move although  one little detail of that is that you  ideally want to stop at the end of a  step otherwise you stop higher or lower  like we've done here so a little bit of  extra code to dig into these kind of  details really helps  once that's working you may notice the  problem  the problem is that we can just pass  through walls we have no collision  detection whatsoever so we'll be  integrating some sort of physics engine  i've been using ammo.js in projects  which is actually the bullet physics  engine a great little open source  physics engine that's good enough for  red dead redemption so it should be more  than good enough for us too this should  have been easy but in reality it wasn't  the docs suck the examples suck  everything sucks including me i kind of  suck at physics  anyway with enough trawling through  forums and stack overflow i was able to  cobble together a working bt kinematic  character controller snippet of code and  the end result that i can run around i  can't pass through walls anymore but i  can slide along them at angles though i  can do all the things that you'd expect  including jump look i can jump that came  along as a nice little bonus  let's add some guns now  i grabbed this gun off of sketch fab it  looked pretty cool so i'm happy there's  not much to making it look first person  shootery just gotta offset it right  until it's roughly where we want it  relative to the camera  after that shooting is up next and the  trick here is to use ammo.js's  raycasting abilities to cast a ray out  from our gun barrel and see what it hits  so whenever i press the mouse button we  fire off a raycast query  and figure out if we hit anything and  then react appropriately depending on  what you hit  i'm not a violent guy though so we'll  make this a paintball gun instead of a  killing people gun  so to get that splat effect we could use  textures but i'm going to hijack 3.js's  shaders to do this procedurally for more  variation  we also use 3.js's deco system to  procedurally create decals from the  underlying geometry  now when i fire off a shot look at that  we do a little splat on the wall and we  get our paintball effect  the cool thing about this is that it  rounds corners nicely and you get  different pattern each time you fire a  shot  easing functions are an awesome little  tool to have in your toolbox as a  programmer  because you can use them to procedurally  add animations to shaders or meshes like  magic i added an exponential impulse to  blend between two rotation quaternions  and the end result is a little firing  recoil when you take a shot  you can of course play with it maybe  recoil more like a machine gun instead  of a pistol up to you  the next step is that i wanted to add  some ui  i took doom eternal as my inspiration  and figured i could pretty much copy  their layout  the nice thing about using javascript is  that i can do this all in html and css  if you want to call that a nice thing  so i threw some styled elements onto the  screen with 3d transforms and box  shadows and sourced a bunch of icons  from google's free library  so we have our health and charge bars  down here i give this guy a charge up  power to run super fast takes down the  bar by just adjusting the width and  changing the color  on the right here's some more ui i kind  of drew this in [ __ ] but the rest of it  is icons and text  at the top i'm actually not quite sure  what this does in doom so i put some  random icons up here  i worked on adding enemies next  i grabbed this little robot thing from  quaternious  comes fully animated already i just  plopped him into the world but he  doesn't do anything right now  we can fix that by adding some crude ai  if we give him his own collision  detection like we have for ourselves and  then throw together a quick finite state  machine that has him in idle walk and  shooting behaviors  we can really quickly and easily give  him a bit of personality  see now he sees me and walks towards me  and even tries to impotently shoot at me  too bad only i have the gun  so yeah of course next thing is you give  him a gun  and i also coded up this forcefield  thing using some voronoi noise  now he's trying to take shots at me and  he has this cool little force field  around him  and if i take a step back here and fire  off a few shots at him his shield kind  of changes color and eventually he dies  and i added this quick dissolve away  effect  pretty happy how easy that was  let's make this look better now  let's make a quick level so what i'll do  here is i'll quickly place down some  cubes and planes and stuff kind of  randomly  we'll also place a few models that i  grabbed off of sketchfab that looked  pretty cool  that looks kind of neat we've got  floating islands at different levels now  and i can run around to jump and play  and do whatever i want  from the corners though you can see that  this doesn't look as good as it could  it's really apparent the shadows suck  they don't extend out very far and we  don't get that nice shadowing on the  distant blocks  cascading shadow maps is typically the  answer to these kind of large landscapes  and as luck would have it there are  options in 3.js that i was able to  integrate  now we peek out from the corner of the  map here it looks great and we have  shadows going on  if you look in the distance you've got  shadows but you've also got shadows  nearby  and both of them have enough detail  i can also inject some of my own shader  code into 3.js for these cubes add some  tri planar mapping and noise that saves  me from actually modeling a level  and that gets me this kind of neat  futuristic look i like it  i also want to get some bloom in here  but 3.js doesn't quite do what i want so  i copy pasted theirs and i'm rewriting  it slightly to be driven by the alpha  channel  that way i can selectively bloom those  light emitting squares and even my shots  have a kind of glow to them but nothing  else does  i also played with the sky a bit what i  did was i combined a cube map of the sky  with a cube map of stars  then i drove the mix between those using  the sky's luminance and applied some  color grading  there's also a bit of rotation using  time and i added that for that small bit  of movement to keep it from being  totally static  i also bloom out the sun manually via  the shader so you get a sort of bleed  over effect in that direction  this also means that those force fields  on the droids they glow a bit now which  improves the look quite a bit i also  took the opportunity to add a little  radial blur effect it has a simple fall  off from the center with a bit of basic  color manipulation  and here it is an action  when activated i contrast the center and  desaturate a bit towards the sides  i don't know see what works for you  there's also a subtle vignette around  the edges of the screen because i like  the look  anyway that's about as far as i feel  like taking this it works i've got the  beginnings of something here maybe  somebody wants to actually take this and  turn it into a real game  so the code will be available on github  look for an announcement on twitter or  when it's available  until next time cheers

